Engine-independent suites (metadata, static assets, viewport layout) run once in desktop Chromium on purpose; do not add them to the other projects. Mobile projects are browser emulations, not physical devices.

## Running

- Routine: `npm run test:e2e:chromium` (desktop and mobile Chromium).
- Full matrix: `npm run test:e2e` (rebuilds `dist/` first, serves it on port 4322).
- One suite or project: `npx playwright test tests/e2e/layout.spec.ts --project=desktop-chromium`.
- Debug: `npm run test:e2e:ui`; last report: `npm run test:e2e:report`.
- Against an existing build without rebuilding: `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BUILD=1 npx playwright test`.
- Browsers are installed once with `npx playwright install chromium firefox webkit`.
- The web server command is `astro preview --port 4322 --ignore-lock`: Astro 7 would otherwise detach the server into the background when run by an agent, and Playwright could not stop it. Keep the flag and the port (4321 is the dev server).

## Engine notes

- WebKit mirrors Safari: plain Tab skips links. Tests that must reach a link in WebKit press `Alt+Tab` (Option+Tab); this is engine behaviour, not a site defect.
- Playwright's WebKit is newer than the Safari visitors run, so it can pass where Safari fails: Safari 27.2 left the navbar call to action's aura lit after the pointer left, from a repaint bug WebKit fixed on 2026-10-09. Check paint and motion changes in Safari itself, and test the mechanism where the engine cannot show the bug (the navigation suite's "fades through its strength" test).
- A Playwright WebKit screenshot breaks a running transition of an `@property` custom property: afterwards the computed value reads the end value while the transition still runs, so the shot shows a finished fade. Sample such fades with `getComputedStyle` across animation frames, or record a video, not with screenshots.
- Frames on a busy runner come far apart, and WebKit on Linux most of all. WebKit starts a transition's clock at the style change but sends `transitionrun` only with the next frame, and a frame callback asked for from that event runs in the same frame. In CI that frame came about 100 ms late, so the industries suite's hover fade read its start colour already 95 ms into the 240 ms fade, read its middle in the same frame, and found the two equal (desktop WebKit, now and then from 2026-11-19, on every attempt in run 45020304737). To read the middle of a fade, do it in the task of the change itself, a `mousemove` listener for a hover, where `getAnimations()` starts the transitions at once. Put each halfway along its own clock and back in that same task, and compare with both end colours as painted bytes (`industries.spec.ts`). A transition that only has to finish can be polled. Frames can also stop for seconds. In a phone profile (three device pixels to the CSS pixel) a 1280 by 9000 window is a 3840 by 27000 pixel surface, and after it shrank Linux WebKit drew no new frame for 4-7 s. Frames that repaint the hero's load animations at that density also came seconds apart. So give a check that needs such a window a test of its own, and bring an element into view at rest (`scrollToRest`) before waiting on its transition (`work.spec.ts`).
- Firefox is Playwright's bundled build. On macOS 27.2 it fails to start ("Could not find profile folder") with or without a sandbox; run the rest of the matrix with `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_FIREFOX=1 npm run test:e2e`, then run Firefox in the Linux container described in `docs/quality-checks.md`, or state in the report that Firefox ran only in CI (Ubuntu). Never skip it silently.
- CI runs on Linux, which still differs from macOS: WebKit styles the page once before the stylesheet arrives, and before Catamaran and Mukta load the generic fallback is DejaVu Sans. After those files load, both platforms draw the same families, but Chromium on Linux hints small type and rounds each letter up to whole pixels, so 10-13px bold text sets up to a sixth wider than on a Mac. Text placed to the pixel needs `text-rendering: geometricPrecision`, as the hero orbit's names have (in the `orbit` utility); without it "WordPress" ran off a 320px screen in CI (2026-11-18). A macOS pass does not show that CI passes; reproduce platform-specific failures in the Linux container.
- Playwright's headless Chromium on Linux fires a trusted `mousemove` at 0,0, with `pointerover`, `mouseover`, and `pointermove`, while a new tab loads its first page, though nothing moved; later pages in the tab get none. The Mac's Chromium does not, nor does full Chromium in new headless mode (`channel: 'chromium'`, how `scripts/perf-audit.mjs` runs Lighthouse). So in CI the first page a test opens has already seen a pointer movement: Tag Manager's container script (`BaseLayout.astro`, added at the first pointer movement) is in the head when `goto` returns, and the element at the top-left corner is hovered. A test that compares two loads in one tab must not count either (CI run 45006399179, 2026-11-22: the smoke suite's light-only test, which now reads painted elements only).
- Playwright 1.65's Firefox (159) crashes the page ("Page crashed", signal 11) when `evaluateAll` runs on a locator inside a region found by its name after anything else has queried that region, such as a count, an expect, or an `evaluate`. To read the boxes of many elements in such a region, run one `evaluate` on their container (`itemBoxes` in `tests/e2e/industry-strip.spec.ts`), or use a CSS locator not chained from the named region.
- A programmatic scroll (`scrollIntoViewIfNeeded`, `scrollTop =`) has not settled when it returns. The navbar's collapse runs on the scroll timeline in the frame after it, and Chromium's scroll anchoring then pulls the offset back by the bar's shrink (16px once past 100px) to keep the content still; CI's Linux WebKit did the same, WebKit on a Mac lets the content rise instead. Inside that 100px range frames alone do not settle Linux WebKit: when its anchoring takes the shrink back, it leaves the 2px the bar then grows back for the next layout of any kind, so the offset reads the same frame after frame and moves later. To compare `window.scrollY` around an action, wait for `document.fonts.ready` (a late font swap re-flows what is above), park the offset past the range with the target wholly on screen, and read it once consecutive frames agree, before and after, as `contact.spec.ts` does (`scrollYAtRest`). Playwright's `click` first scrolls a target that pokes even half a pixel past the viewport into view, and desktop WebKit's `scrollIntoViewIfNeeded` brings a target from below only to the viewport's bottom edge, where the other projects centre it (CI runs of 2026-11-21: `contact.spec.ts` in every project, then in desktop WebKit only). The navigation suite's `scrollTo` waits two frames, enough for the sizes it checks within bounds. Before a hover or click on something Playwright must scroll to, call `scrollToRest` (`tests/e2e/scroll.ts`): a pointer aimed between the scroll and that adjustment misses. A hover ended above a footer link at the page's foot, and in WebKit the adjustment cancelled the smooth scroll a link's click had just started (on a Mac and in the Linux container, 2026-11-22; GitHub's runner passed both).
- A scroll-driven animation's timing is a share of its scroll range, not a time: Chromium and WebKit give `getComputedTiming().endTime` as a percentage (`CSSUnitValue`, `NaN` to `Number()`), Firefox as milliseconds of a notional 100-second timeline, so the navbar's 100px range read as 8.4 s on the short contact page (CI run of 2026-11-21, `motion.spec.ts`). Tell such animations apart by `animation.timeline !== document.timeline`, never by their timing.
- WebKit applies `:focus-visible` to the button `showModal()` focuses, and to one a script focuses afterwards, even when the visitor's last act was a tap or a click (WebKit bug 247416, on iOS and macOS; Playwright's WebKit shows the first case). Chromium and Firefox do not. `Navbar.astro` moves focus without the ring after a pointer (`focus({ focusVisible: false })` after a `blur()`, which Safari 18.4 and Firefox honour and Chromium does not need), and `navigation.spec.ts` checks both the bare pointer case and the ringed keyboard case. Assert a ring with `matches(':focus-visible')` and the computed `outline-style`, not with a screenshot.
- Under `javaScriptEnabled: false`, never assert with `toHaveJSProperty`: it is the one matcher Playwright evaluates in the page's own script world, which Firefox does not provide while JavaScript is off, so the expect never answers and the test runs into its timeout (CI run 44825131550, 2026-11-20, in `detail-pages.spec.ts`). Every other matcher runs in Playwright's utility world and works. Assert what the visitor sees instead: a `details` that has opened carries `open=""` (`toHaveAttribute('open', '')`) and its answer `toBeVisible()`.

## Writing tests

- Add a focused spec for each new interactive island or form (`tests/e2e/<feature>.spec.ts`). A test that walks every built route sets its timeout from the route count (`test.setTimeout(routes.length * …)`), as the a11y, seo, rendering, motion, layout, and page-hero suites do, so adding pages never trips the 30-second default. Page-level metadata, layout, and accessibility are already covered by the site-wide suites, so do not duplicate those assertions.
- Locate elements by role and accessible name (`getByRole('button', { name: /send/i })`), then by label or text. Avoid CSS selectors tied to Tailwind classes.
- Use web-first assertions (`await expect(locator).toBeVisible()`); never `waitForTimeout`.
- Import `test` and `expect` from `./fixtures`, never from `@playwright/test`: the fixture's context stands in for Google Tag Manager. A context a test makes for itself from `browser` must route `https://www.googletagmanager.com/**` the same way.
- Test behaviour visible to the user: navigation opens and closes with keyboard, form validation messages appear and are associated with fields, filters change the list. Do not test third-party services; for Netlify Forms assert the hidden `form-name` input and attributes, and answer the form's POST from the test with `page.route` (`answerWith` in `tests/e2e/contact.spec.ts`) rather than letting it reach a server; Calendly is stood in for the same way, and Google Tag Manager by the shared fixture.
- Keep tests independent and deterministic; no shared mutable state between tests.
- Mark temporary skips with a reason: `test.skip(condition, 'reason')`, and remove them before finishing.

## Screenshots

`reports/screenshots/` holds inspection screenshots written by the layout suite and by manual checks. They are gitignored and are not visual-regression baselines; look at them and describe what you saw. If visual regression testing is adopted later, baselines must be reviewed on every change, never regenerated to make a failure disappear.

## When a check fails

Read the failing step's name (route, width, or action), reproduce locally with `--ui` if needed, fix the page rather than loosening the assertion. Thresholds in the SEO, layout, and accessibility suites change only with a documented decision in `docs/quality-checks.md`.
